<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Nene</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Nene rises in Northamptonshire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a> and flows north-east through Northampton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> and Wisbech to the Wash. Its valley was a corridor of settlement from prehistoric times, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and to the abbey of Peterborough.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-nene.jpg" alt="River Nene" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stavros1 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kislingbury_Watermill_on_The_River_Nene_30th_March_2008_(10).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The River Nene flows through Northamptonshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and into the Wash. One of its sources lies near Arbury Hill in Northamptonshire, and its mouth is at Lutton Marsh. It is about 105 miles (169 km) long and, with its tributaries, drains some 2,270 square kilometres. The main tributaries include the River Ise, Wootton Brook, Willow Brook and Harper's Brook. Below Northampton the river runs through a broad, flat valley formed by meltwater at the end of the Ice Age, with floodplains and the gravel deposits that later became gravel pits. The river is tidal for 45 kilometres, up to the Dog-in-a-Doublet sluice near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a>, and forms part of the boundary between Cambridgeshire and Norfolk.</p><p>The origin of the name is unknown. The earliest forms date from the 10th century, and the toponymist Victor Watts called the name certainly pre-English and possibly pre-Celtic. The pronunciation changes along the river, with Nene rhyming with 'keen' around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> and with 'ten' in Northampton.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Along its banks lie traces of the Roman past, among them the remains of the Roman town at Irchester and the point near Oundle where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermine-street">Ermine Street</a> over the river in the first century. Hardwater Watermill near Doddington is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> and ground flour for almost a thousand years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a>, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, is said to have used it as a hiding place in 1164 after escaping from Northampton Castle and fleeing down the river.</p><p>Efforts to make the river navigable began with surveys in 1567 and 1606. An Act of Parliament of 1713 found no taker, and a second Act in 1724 allowed work in stages at the contractor's cost. Robert Wright and Thomas Squire finished the stretch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> to Oundle by 1730 and the section to Thrapston by late 1737. Below Peterborough Bishop Morton built Morton's Leam, a straight channel to Wisbech, in the 1470s, and the Bedford Level Corporation cut Smith's Leam in 1728. By the time the Nene Catchment Board took over after the Land Drainage Act 1930 the river was described as in decay that had no equal. The board rebuilt the locks, and the floods of 1947 showed the benefit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is navigable for 88 miles (142 km) from Northampton to the Wash, through locks that take boats up to 78 by 13 feet, and most leisure use lies between Northampton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a>. Its valley is largely occupied by mature gravel-pit lakes, and Titchmarsh Nature Reserve, designated in 1989, lies beside it north of Thrapston. Serious floods struck Wisbech in January 1978 and Northampton and other riverside towns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Nene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near the <b>River Nene</b>, Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick, and Edward, Earl of March, defeated the Lancastrian army of King Henry VI and took the king prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Middle Angles were a group of peoples in the east Midlands, around the Fens and the <b>Nene</b> and Welland valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-middle-angles">Kingdom of the Middle Angles</a></li></ul></section>
